Task: CameraRig — orthographic ~45° tilt, medium framing (car ≈15% of viewport height), smooth follow, frustum refit on any orientation/resize (manual verify on portrait + landscape)

Summary of changes:
- src/game/camera.ts: replaced createCamera/updateCameraFrustum with
  createCameraRig(aspect) returning { camera, focus, setTarget, snapTo,
  update, resize }. Framing constants are derived, not tuned by hand:
  CAR_LENGTH 0.9 / CAR_VIEW_SHARE 0.17 => VIEW_HEIGHT ≈ 5.3 world units, so
  the car will occupy the spec'd 15-20% of viewport height once vehicles
  exist. Fixed ~45° tilt via equal vertical/horizontal camera offset;
  follow uses exponential smoothing (tau = 0.35s) so a given elapsed time
  produces the same travel regardless of frame rate; zero/negative deltas
  are ignored; resize refits the frustum with a square fallback for a
  degenerate (zero-size/hidden) container.
- src/main.ts: builds the rig, opens on the primary spawn point (the camera
  focus is real map data even before the car exists), refits on
  ResizeObserver, and advances the rig from the render loop's frame delta.
- src/game/camera.test.ts: rewritten as 12 tests, written first and
  confirmed failing (module-not-found) before implementation: framing share
  equals the spec range, constant vertical extent across landscape/portrait,
  35-55° tilt, focus projects to viewport centre while following, eased
  (non-snapping) start, >95% of the gap closed in 1.5s, frame-rate
  independence, degenerate-aspect fallback, and instant snapTo.

Verification:
- Automated: pnpm check clean, pnpm typecheck clean, CI=true pnpm test
  → 6 files / 57 tests; camera.ts at 100% statements and branches
  (project 99.3%).
- Manual, production preview: landscape shows medium framing centred on the
  spawn point with neighbouring houses and the cross street readable.
- Manual, portrait: emulated a 520x900 container (canvas CSS 520x900,
  drawing buffer 780x1350 at DPR 1.5) — the frustum refit kept the vertical
  world extent identical and narrowed the horizontal extent, exactly as the
  any-orientation rule requires.
- No console output in either orientation.
